  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": "pdf-p43",
      "document_id": "G21I",
      "pdf_page": 43,
      "page_markdown": "documents/G21I.md#page-43",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "text": "The TOGAF Opportunities & Solutions phase identifies delivery vehicles (projects, programs, or portfolios) that effectively deliver the Target Architecture defined in previous phases. It reviews the target business objectives and capabilities, consolidates the gaps from Phases B to D, and organizes groups of building blocks to address these capabilities. It then generates an outline Implementation and Migration Strategy.",
          "source_block_ids": [
            "G21I:p0043:b003",
            "G21I:p0043:b004",
            "G21I:p0043:b005",
            "G21I:p0043:b006",
            "G21I:p0043:b007"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "There are some nuances between a cloud-native solution and a microservices-based solution. Microservices are a type of cloud-native solution, but they can have non-cloud deployments as well. Cloud-native is exclusively designed for the cloud. The intent of cloud-native is to optimally use cloud- scale architecture and cloud services in the solution. Microservices have other constraints and motivations; refer to The Open Group White Paper: Microservices Architecture [W169] for a deeper understanding. The commonalities are that they are both very modular and highly distributed deployments. They are elastically scalable and resilient.",
          "source_block_ids": [
            "G21I:p0043:b008",
            "G21I:p0043:b009",
            "G21I:p0043:b010",
            "G21I:p0043:b011",
            "G21I:p0043:b012",
            "G21I:p0043:b013",
            "G21I:p0043:b014"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "selection": "same-page-context"
    },
    {
      "id": "pdf-p44",
      "document_id": "G21I",
      "pdf_page": 44,
      "page_markdown": "documents/G21I.md#page-44",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "text": "To successfully embrace cloud-native and MSA requires significant organizational transformation. The target state demands single team ownership of each module (microservice) and a lean, federated governance structure. Each team will autonomously own the entire lifecycle of the microservice, and there is a need for coordination to enable all capabilities of the desired target state solution.",
          "source_block_ids": [
            "G21I:p0044:b003",
            "G21I:p0044:b004",
            "G21I:p0044:b005",
            "G21I:p0044:b006"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "This new paradigm presents a good opportunity for adopting an Agile/DevOps methodology; see The Open Group White Paper: Benefits of DevOps Methodology for Microservices Solutions [W196].",
          "source_block_ids": [
            "G21I:p0044:b007",
            "G21I:p0044:b008"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "selection": "same-page-context"
    },
